window.RodinBenchmarkSearch=[{"path": "Rodin/Adaptation", "title": "Adaptation", "url": "Rodin/Adaptation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Adaptation", "brief": "Mesh adaptation and deformation operations.", "text": "SWIFT:: Adapt and SWIFT:: Problem fit mesh interfaces to level sets while controlling element distortion. DeformationMap and CellDeformation describe mapped geometry for evaluating deformed cells and fields. These components support moving-interface calculations and body-fitted mesh updates.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Advection", "title": "Advection", "url": "Rodin/Advection/index.html", "display": "Modules/Advection", "brief": "Transport and advection of fields.", "text": "Lagrangian advances scalar fields on unstructured meshes by tracing characteristics and projecting the transported field into a finite element space. It is used to transport level sets and other evolving fields; boundary policies control the treatment of characteristics that reach the mesh boundary.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Alert", "title": "Alert", "url": "Rodin/Alert/index.html", "display": "Modules/Alert", "brief": "Contextual exceptions and diagnostic reporting.", "text": "Alert:: Exception and Alert:: MemberFunctionException report errors with contextual diagnostics. Alert:: Info and Alert:: Warning provide nonfatal messages through the same stream interface. These classes communicate invalid inputs, unavailable operations, and solver or mesh-processing status.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly", "title": "Assembly", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly", "brief": "Global assembly of discrete operators.", "text": "Assembly:: Sequential evaluates local integrals and accumulates global matrices and vectors; Assembly:: Default selects the configured assembly strategy. BilinearFormAssemblyInput supplies spaces and integrators, and ConstraintMap expands degree-of-freedom constraints when assembling constrained or coupled problems.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Constrained", "title": "Constrained", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Constrained/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Constrained", "brief": "Warmed constrained native assembly with manufactured numerical checks.", "text": "Warmed constrained native assembly with manufactured numerical checks. Measured operation The public assembly operation is repeated on a fixed sparse layout. Manufactured solutions and independent reference calculations qualify the matrix and load before timing; repeated-call checks detect altered results. Constraint processing belongs to the measured assembly operation, while fixture preparation and reference construction do not. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinConstrainedAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Convection", "title": "Convection", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Convection/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Convection", "brief": "Measures assembly consumers of the H1 directional-gradient kernel.", "text": "Measures assembly consumers of the H1 directional-gradient kernel. Measured operation Measures one warmed assembly operation after complete qualification. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinConvectionAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/CoupledReactionDiffusion", "title": "CoupledReactionDiffusion", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/CoupledReactionDiffusion/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/CoupledReactionDiffusion", "brief": "Full two-field reaction--diffusion assembly, separately from solving.", "text": "Full two-field reaction--diffusion assembly, separately from solving. Measured operation Measures the public coupled problem assembly and block completion. Architecture The shared workload defines two diffusion blocks, two diagonal and two off-diagonal reaction terms, two loads and both Dirichlet traces. Original local operator kernels independently qualify the complete matrix. Loads share their production integration path and are also checked through the analytic manufactured patch, not an independent load algorithm. The manufactured block vector is gathered by the public problem assembly, rather than constructing an assumed component ordering. An omitted-coupling problem retains the original sources and traces. Its residual is normalized by the independently assembled omitted mass action. Only essential output rows are masked: prescribed input coefficients still contribute to the missing physical reaction. Logical boundary DOFs and the problem's test offsets determine these rows. No coordinate tests are used. Repeated full assembly is timed after setup; no solve or numerical guard is timed. The operation includes block offsets, insertion, constraints and initial-guess gathering. It is opt-in and is not a CTest timing gate. real test-side weighting, exercising generic physical-gradient preparation. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinCoupledReactionDiffusionAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Helmholtz", "title": "Helmholtz", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Helmholtz/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Helmholtz", "brief": "Complete constrained Helmholtz assembly, separately from solving.", "text": "Complete constrained Helmholtz assembly, separately from solving. Measured operation Measures repeated full assembly of the shared Helmholtz formulations. The physical forms, exact coefficients, quadrature and grid sizes match HelmholtzWorkload. Setup, interpolation, the original-kernel reference and numerical checks precede timing. Public Problem::assemble includes volume kernels, matrix/load insertion and Dirichlet processing; no solve is performed. Repeated calls measure the warmed fixed-layout operation, not process-first allocation or changing coefficients. The manufactured equation \\(Ax_\\star=b\\) and the full matrix/load comparison are checked before and after timing. A wrong coefficient vector must fail the equation; the complex-shifted case must retain a positive imaginary diagonal sum. These are untimed correctness guards, not CTest registrations or performance gates. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinHelmholtzAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Mass", "title": "Mass", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Mass/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Mass", "brief": "Full Eigen mass/reaction operator and projection-load assembly.", "text": "Full Eigen mass/reaction operator and projection-load assembly. Measured operation Times one public operator or load assembly, with independent oracles. Architecture Setup, independent geometry, original-loop assembly, constant-field energy and projection identities are untimed. Repeated assembly must replace output. A doubled reaction coefficient is checked after timing to detect stale expression values. No projection solve or constraint elimination is timed. The workload parameter supplies the form without duplicating assembly or qualification. Its default retains the original right-weighted reaction. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMassAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/MassOperandOrder", "title": "MassOperandOrder", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/MassOperandOrder/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/MassOperandOrder", "brief": "Public H1 scalar mass assembly with both coefficient operand orders.", "text": "Public H1 scalar mass assembly with both coefficient operand orders. Measured operation Times one public operator or load assembly, with independent oracles. Architecture Setup, independent geometry, original-loop assembly, constant-field energy and projection identities are untimed. Repeated assembly must replace output. A doubled reaction coefficient is checked after timing to detect stale expression values. No projection solve or constraint elimination is timed. The workload parameter supplies the form without duplicating assembly or qualification. Its default retains the original right-weighted reaction. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMassOperandOrderAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/MatrixMass", "title": "MatrixMass", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/MatrixMass/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/MatrixMass", "brief": "Public matrix-field reaction operator and projection-load assembly.", "text": "Public matrix-field reaction operator and projection-load assembly. Measured operation Times one public operator or load assembly, with independent oracles. Architecture Setup, independent geometry, original-loop assembly, constant-field energy and projection identities are untimed. Repeated assembly must replace output. A doubled reaction coefficient is checked after timing to detect stale expression values. No projection solve or constraint elimination is timed. The workload parameter supplies the form without duplicating assembly or qualification. Its default retains the original right-weighted reaction. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMatrixMassAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/MatrixValuedForms", "title": "MatrixValuedForms", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/MatrixValuedForms/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/MatrixValuedForms", "brief": "Matrix-valued global assembly compared with nine scalar blocks.", "text": "Matrix-valued global assembly compared with nine scalar blocks. Measured operation A three-by-three matrix form is assembled directly and as nine scalar forms. The benchmark checks both sparse and triplet assembly against the scalar-block reference before recording repeated assembly. This measures the whole assembly consumer of spatial-matrix arithmetic, including global insertion, rather than the cost of a single matrix product. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialMatrixBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/NonlinearPoisson", "title": "NonlinearPoisson", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/NonlinearPoisson/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/NonlinearPoisson", "brief": "Prescribed-state semilinear residual and tangent assembly.", "text": "Prescribed-state semilinear residual and tangent assembly. Measured operation Measures the volume assembly used by semilinear Poisson Newton steps. The benchmark separates residual and tangent costs at a fixed discrete state. Architecture On the unit box, a public DOF interpolation prescribes \\(q=s(1+x_0)\\). Separate public forms assemble \\(R(q;v)=\\int\\nabla q\\cdot\\nabla v+(q+q^3)v\\) and \\(J(q;w,v)=\\int\\nabla w\\cdot\\nabla v+(1+3q^2)wv\\). Untimed gates compare the tangent with the original quadrature loop, check analytic actions, and differentiate the assembled residual. Warm replacement assembly is timed; a post-timing state change checks cache invalidation. There are no constraints, boundary integrals, source terms, nonlinear iterations, or linear solves in this scope. Curved Q2 cases use the existing volume-preserving quadratic shear in dimensions two and three. Its first coordinate is unchanged, so the same affine state and analytic actions apply without a geometry-dependent reference solve. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinNonlinearPoissonAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Physics", "title": "Physics", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Physics/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Physics", "brief": "Warmed real P1/P2 physics operator assembly on all geometries.", "text": "Warmed real P1/P2 physics operator assembly on all geometries. Measured operation Measures repeated assembly of a fixed physical bilinear form. Architecture: mesh, space, and form construction precede timing. An interpolated affine field supplies an independent analytic energy oracle. Two untimed assemblies verify replacement rather than accumulation. The timed loop includes the complete public assembly operation; the final operator is checked outside timing against the initial operator and energy. No solve, constraint elimination, or mesh construction is timed. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPhysicsAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/PhysicsStages", "title": "PhysicsStages", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/PhysicsStages/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/PhysicsStages", "brief": "Overlapping sequential assembly-stage measurements.", "text": "Overlapping sequential assembly-stage measurements. Measured operation Separates local evaluation from canonical sparse assembly costs. Binding visits all cells and binds each integrator. Kernel additionally evaluates every local entry. Triplets uses the library's sequential assembler, including binding, evaluation, and scatter. Finalize replaces an Eigen sparse matrix using previously generated triplets. These scopes overlap and must not be added or interpreted as disjoint percentages. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPhysicsStageBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Poisson", "title": "Poisson", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Poisson/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Poisson", "brief": "Poisson assembly benchmark coverage", "text": "Poisson assembly benchmark coverage Measured operation Benchmarks assembly of a scalar P1 Poisson problem on a 16x16 triangular grid, with both unit diffusion and explicit constant coefficient forms. The cases isolate form assembly cost from mesh and finite-element-space setup. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Assembly/Stokes", "title": "Stokes", "url": "Rodin/Assembly/Stokes/index.html", "display": "Modules/Assembly/Stokes", "brief": "Measures the StokesAssembly workload through its public library pipeline.", "text": "Measures the StokesAssembly workload through its public library pipeline. Measured operation Measures repeated public Stokes assembly, including constraints and global pressure-mean completion. Setup checks the exact affine patch residual and rejects wrong-field and missing-pressure controls without factorizing the system. Every timed assembly is checked against the exact field outside timing. Recorded results Each case retains its mesh, finite element spaces, quadrature and iteration policy. Solver-specific coefficient and solve checks belong to the solver benchmark. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together. The missing-pressure control is scaled by the independently assembled forcing load on unconstrained velocity test DOFs, not by Dirichlet-elimination entries in the full right-hand side. Since the affine field has zero viscous divergence, the omitted residual must equal the negative load within the full-RHS-normalized 10 \u221211 residual tolerance, with relative magnitude above 10 \u22124 . The original full-RHS-normalized counter is retained for comparison.", "target": "RodinStokesAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Context", "title": "Context", "url": "Rodin/Context/index.html", "display": "Modules/Context", "brief": "Execution contexts for local and distributed computation.", "text": "Local selects local execution, and Context:: MPI carries the environment and communicator for distributed execution. Mesh, connectivity, and finite element specializations use these context types to select their data distribution and backend behavior while preserving the same mathematical interfaces.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Distance", "title": "Distance", "url": "Rodin/Distance/index.html", "display": "Modules/Distance", "brief": "Distance-field computations.", "text": "Distance:: Eikonal constructs distance fields through fast marching, while Distance:: Poisson and Distance:: SignedPoisson provide PDE-based alternatives. These models convert boundary or level-set information into distance approximations for shape optimization, interface processing, and mesh adaptation.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Eikonal", "title": "Eikonal", "url": "Rodin/Eikonal/index.html", "display": "Modules/Eikonal", "brief": "Eikonal equations and distance propagation.", "text": "FMM implements fast marching on simplicial meshes, propagating arrival times from prescribed seed vertices. It provides the underlying distance propagation used by level-set reinitialization and distance-field construction, with a priority queue controlling the order of vertex updates.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/FormLanguage", "title": "Form language", "url": "Rodin/FormLanguage/index.html", "display": "Modules/FormLanguage", "brief": "Typed expressions for constructing variational forms.", "text": "Base supplies the common expression-node interface, Traits describes ranges and algebraic properties, and List groups polymorphic nodes. They underpin the typed variational expressions used to compose integrals and operators, and provide extension points for new function and form types.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry", "title": "Geometry", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry", "brief": "Meshes, geometric transformations, and mapped evaluation.", "text": "Mesh<Context:: MPI> and getConnectivity().compute(d1, d2) describe mesh entities and their incidence relations. Polytope , Geometry:: Point , and PolytopeTransformation connect reference elements to physical geometry for integration, field evaluation, mesh extraction, and deformation.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/BulkP1Jacobian", "title": "BulkP1Jacobian", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/BulkP1Jacobian/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/BulkP1Jacobian", "brief": "Bulk P1 geometry contractions and their mapped-quadrature consumer.", "text": "Bulk P1 geometry contractions and their mapped-quadrature consumer. Measured operation Isolates the bulk geometry operation used by public assembly. Architecture The actual native P1 element and its existing reference table are used. Bulk contractions overwrite retained output matrices; OwnedQuadrature constructs/replaces the mapped points used by variational integrators. PreparedPoint constructs and destroys stack points from untimed prepared Jacobians. RetainedPoints clears and refills a reserved point vector from the same inputs, without timed vector allocation or Jacobian evaluation. These scopes expose public source operations, not additive exclusive-time fractions: their storage lifetimes and cache traffic differ. No scope includes mesh construction, reference-table initialization, analytic guards, operator assembly or solving. Full assembly is measured separately by its owning Assembly targets on matched quadrature orders. Affine controls represent \\(x=b+A\\widehat x\\), with nonzero mixed components, so the independent Jacobian is \\(A\\). Perturbed controls exercise the same native basis without assuming an affine Jacobian on tensor-product, wedge or rational pyramid cells. Simplex P1 maps remain affine. Every result must exactly reproduce the original pointwise contraction and its dimensions; affine cases additionally satisfy an independent analytic roundoff budget. Wrong entries or dimensions must be rejected. No new cache or alternative production geometry path is added. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinBulkP1JacobianBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/Connectivity", "title": "Connectivity", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/Connectivity/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/Connectivity", "brief": "Mesh incidence construction and retained connectivity queries.", "text": "Mesh incidence construction and retained connectivity queries. Measured operation Cold and warm construction of incidence relations, transposes and intersections is measured on segment, triangular, quadrilateral, tetrahedral, wedge, pyramidal and hexahedral grids. The reported entity counts distinguish first computation from reuse of an already prepared relation. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinGeometryConnectivityBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/MappedPoint", "title": "MappedPoint", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/MappedPoint/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/MappedPoint", "brief": "Separates mapped-point caches, geometry kernels and quadrature construction.", "text": "Separates mapped-point caches, geometry kernels and quadrature construction. Measured operation Times a selected geometry scope and checks its complete output. per factor (Gaussian family), and timing iterations. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMappedPointBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/MappedQuadratureCache", "title": "MappedQuadratureCache", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/MappedQuadratureCache/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/MappedQuadratureCache", "brief": "Mesh-owned mapped-quadrature lookup along the assembly call tree.", "text": "Mesh-owned mapped-quadrature lookup along the assembly call tree. Measured operation Measures cold index storage separately from owned mapped quadratures. FirstCell constructs one mapped quadrature per live formula; CellSweep constructs one per cell. Cache scopes include index initialization, dense empty-slot allocation, publication and destruction. Owned scopes construct and destroy quadratures directly, without retaining cell-wide storage. These are complete lifecycles, not isolated allocation latencies. All transformations are warmed outside timing, with fixed live formulas. One-formula scopes retain warm reference tables. Twenty-formula scopes exceed the eight-entry reference store and include its retabulation policy; they must not be interpreted as pure mapped-index allocation measurements. Comparing FirstCell across mesh sizes exposes the cost of empty slots at fixed mapped-point count. Comparing full sweeps instead includes retention versus immediate destruction, and must not be interpreted as the same memory-lifetime contract. Slot counts describe the source representation, not measured resident bytes. No collective or field solve is performed. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMappedQuadratureCacheBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/ParametricTransformation", "title": "ParametricTransformation", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/ParametricTransformation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/ParametricTransformation", "brief": "Parametric-map and Jacobian evaluation across reference geometries.", "text": "Parametric-map and Jacobian evaluation across reference geometries. Measured operation Given a cell map \\(F_K\\), the cases evaluate either \\(F_K(\\widehat x)\\) or its derivative \\(DF_K(\\widehat x)\\). Geometry family and representation degree are independent inputs. Repeated point evaluations isolate map and Jacobian work from global assembly. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinGeometryParametricTransformationBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/ReferenceGeometry", "title": "ReferenceGeometry", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/ReferenceGeometry/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/ReferenceGeometry", "brief": "Paired reference-table and pointwise geometry Jacobian measurements.", "text": "Paired reference-table and pointwise geometry Jacobian measurements. Measured operation Measures bulk Jacobians against the original pointwise fallback. Both paths evaluate \\(J_{ji}(\\widehat x_q)= \\sum_a X_{ja}\\partial_i\\phi_a(\\widehat x_q)\\) with the same vertex basis and accumulation order. The pointwise measurement explicitly calls the base transformation's bulk fallback; no second production path is added. Setup, warmup and exact entry comparisons are outside timing. Repeated calls retain output-vector capacity. Physical Jacobians are timed, but no physical-coordinate evaluation, FE assembly or solve is timed. Results are local diagnostics, not CTest or CI gates. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinReferenceGeometryBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/TransformationLifecycle", "title": "TransformationLifecycle", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/TransformationLifecycle/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/TransformationLifecycle", "brief": "Measures geometric transformation construction and value lifetimes.", "text": "Measures geometric transformation construction and value lifetimes. Measured operation Measures identity-map construction without geometric evaluation. Construct measures one map. ConstructCopy and ConstructMove each construct a source and a destination; source construction is deliberately included, rather than hidden behind per-iteration timing pauses. Both addresses escape through mutable pointer barriers. No heap allocation, mesh, formula, assembly or solver is included. Dimensions and the identity Jacobian are qualified outside timing, including the empty zero-dimensional map. construction/move. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinTransformationLifecycleBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Geometry/UniformGrid", "title": "UniformGrid", "url": "Rodin/Geometry/UniformGrid/index.html", "display": "Modules/Geometry/UniformGrid", "brief": "Uniform-grid benchmark coverage", "text": "Uniform-grid benchmark coverage Measured operation Benchmarks construction of triangular uniform grids at increasing resolutions. The cases measure mesh generation scaling for the geometry layer without adding finite-element or solver work. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinGeometryUniformGridBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Heart", "title": "Heart", "url": "Rodin/Heart/index.html", "display": "Modules/Heart", "brief": "Cardiac models and their coupled state evolution.", "text": "CCMLC2014T advances a reduced ventricular model through a cardiac cycle. HolzapfelReducedLaw and CCMLC2014PassiveLaw supply its reduced passive energy and stress response. The module supports cardiac-cycle studies and constitutive parameter calibration before larger three-dimensional simulations.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Hilbert", "title": "Hilbert", "url": "Rodin/Hilbert/index.html", "display": "Modules/Hilbert", "brief": "Hilbert-space formulations and regularization.", "text": "getTrialFunction() / getTestFunction() solves a regularized extension problem that converts a linear functional into a smooth field over the mesh. It is commonly used to extend boundary shape derivatives into volume descent directions, with a configurable length scale controlling smoothing and boundary conditions constraining the extension.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/IO", "title": "IO", "url": "Rodin/IO/index.html", "display": "Modules/IO", "brief": "Reading and writing meshes, fields, and associated data.", "text": "MeshLoader and MeshPrinter import and export meshes; GridFunctionLoader and GridFunctionPrinter transfer discrete fields. Format-specific backends support MFEM, MEDIT, and HDF5 data, while IO:: XDMF writes meshes, multiple fields, and time series for visualization.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/IO/MeshIO", "title": "MeshIO", "url": "Rodin/IO/MeshIO/index.html", "display": "Modules/IO/MeshIO", "brief": "Mesh I/O benchmark coverage", "text": "Mesh I/O benchmark coverage Measured operation Benchmarks loading representative MEDIT meshes from disk. The cases protect the cost model for mesh parser setup and repeated mesh-file ingestion used by examples and regression workflows. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinIOBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Location", "title": "Location", "url": "Rodin/Location/index.html", "display": "Modules/Location", "brief": "Spatial indexing and geometric search.", "text": "AABB builds a bounding-volume hierarchy to locate physical coordinates in a mesh and recover the containing cell and reference coordinates. The resulting Geometry:: Point can be used for field sampling and evaluation at arbitrary locations. The locator must be rebuilt when the mesh geometry or topology changes.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Location/AABB", "title": "AABB", "url": "Rodin/Location/AABB/index.html", "display": "Modules/Location/AABB", "brief": "AABB construction and point location across cell geometries.", "text": "AABB construction and point location across cell geometries. Measured operation An axis-aligned bounding-box hierarchy reduces geometric candidates before inverse-map tests decide membership. Construction and point-query cases distinguish the index lifecycle from retained searches. Cell families and mesh sizes remain explicit parameters, allowing candidate selection and geometric evaluation costs to be assessed together. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinLocationBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Location/AABB/Workload", "title": "Workload", "url": "Rodin/Location/AABB/Workload/index.html", "display": "Modules/Location/AABB/Workload", "brief": "Paired AABB queries with and without projection pruning.", "text": "Paired AABB queries with and without projection pruning. Measured operation The CSV workload varies cell geometry, representation degree, grid resolution and distortion. Interior, shared-interface and nearby-miss queries compare pruning enabled and disabled on identical points; Point cells omit the inapplicable shared-interface family. Independent membership and returned-point checks precede measurement. Timing boundary Query blocks alternate the paired configurations and report median process-CPU nanoseconds per query. Separate repeated constructions report nanoseconds per index build. Geometry and query preparation are excluded. The original sample counts and block-duration policy are retained; --list enumerates the exact configurations without performing numerical work. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinAABBWorkload", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/MMG", "title": "MMG", "url": "Rodin/MMG/index.html", "display": "Modules/MMG", "brief": "Mesh adaptation through the MMG integration.", "text": "MMG:: Mesh:: Build() connects Rodin geometry to MMG. MMG:: Adapt performs metric-driven adaptation, MMG:: Optimizer improves mesh quality, and noSplit(ref) fits or extracts an isosurface. These workflows support remeshing and interface reconstruction in shape optimization and evolving-geometry simulations.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/MPI", "title": "MPI", "url": "Rodin/MPI/index.html", "display": "Modules/MPI", "brief": "Distributed meshes, finite element spaces, and search operations.", "text": "Context:: MPI supplies the distributed execution context, while Mesh<Context:: MPI> manages distributed mesh entities and connectivity. Sharder partitions mesh data for transfer between ranks. Distributed finite element spaces, point location, and assembly build on these components for multi-process PDE calculations.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/MPI/Location", "title": "Location", "url": "Rodin/MPI/Location/index.html", "display": "Modules/MPI/Location", "brief": "Distributed point location.", "text": "Distributed point location. MPI location queries combine rank-local spatial searches with the communication needed to identify a containing cell. Partition ownership and rank count are part of the workload; local and distributed timings are distinguished.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/MPI/Location/AABB", "title": "AABB", "url": "Rodin/MPI/Location/AABB/index.html", "display": "Modules/MPI/Location/AABB", "brief": "Local, subset and distributed AABB query paths.", "text": "Local, subset and distributed AABB query paths. Measured operation Paired workloads cover the reference-cell families through local search, a restricted candidate subset and MPI location. Rank counts remain part of the configuration. The drivers qualify returned membership and point data before timing, separating communication and partition effects from geometric correctness. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMPIAABBBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/MPI/Variational", "title": "Variational", "url": "Rodin/MPI/Variational/index.html", "display": "Modules/MPI/Variational", "brief": "Distributed finite-element spaces.", "text": "Distributed finite-element spaces. Distributed spaces partition global degrees of freedom into owned and halo coefficients. Construction prepares local indexing and exchanges the metadata required for later evaluation and assembly. The benchmarks separate that preparation from creation of the underlying mesh.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/MPI/Variational/Construction", "title": "Construction", "url": "Rodin/MPI/Variational/Construction/index.html", "display": "Modules/MPI/Variational/Construction", "brief": "Distributed finite-element-space construction benchmarks.", "text": "Distributed finite-element-space construction benchmarks. Measured operation Mesh creation is excluded from the timed region. Each iteration times the slowest rank's P0 or vector P1 construction, including the ghost-DOF exchange and reverse-index construction. Fixed iteration counts keep MPI collectives aligned across ranks. Run with two or more MPI ranks. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMPIConstructionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math", "title": "Math", "url": "Rodin/Math/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math", "brief": "Spatial arithmetic, matrix operations, and sparse linear algebra.", "text": "SpatialVector<T> and SpatialMatrix<T> support small spatial calculations without heap allocation. Vector<T> , Matrix<T> , and their fixed-size counterparts provide Eigen-compatible algebra; LinearSystem<Operator, Vector> groups system data and constraint elimination. These types support geometric mappings, constitutive calculations, assembly, and linear solves.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/LinearSystem", "title": "LinearSystem", "url": "Rodin/Math/LinearSystem/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/LinearSystem", "brief": "Linear-system operations.", "text": "Linear-system operations. The algebraic system \\(Ax=b\\) couples a stored operator and right-hand side. Elimination workloads prescribe selected coefficients and measure the corresponding matrix and load transformation, including structural growth when required.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/LinearSystem/Eliminate", "title": "Eliminate", "url": "Rodin/Math/LinearSystem/Eliminate/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/LinearSystem/Eliminate", "brief": "Serial sparse elimination by system size and constraint distribution.", "text": "Serial sparse elimination by system size and constraint distribution. Measured operation Measures arbitrary directed CSC storage, including missing diagonals. # Architecture Upper-band columns have no guaranteed stored transpose. Every seventh diagonal and every seventeenth column are omitted in the second pattern. Prescribed coefficients define an algebraically exact solution. Independent projectors check the entire result before and after measurement. # Implementation Each invocation receives an exact-capacity copy of the original CSC matrix outside timing. Scratch and missing-diagonal growth belong to the measured call; assembly, restoration and verification do not. The calling thread's CPU time is recorded, with no worker pool. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSparseEliminationBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/Scalar", "title": "Scalar", "url": "Rodin/Math/Scalar/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/Scalar", "brief": "Scalar contractions.", "text": "Scalar contractions. Scalar-loop reductions provide independent comparators for spatial and Eigen expressions. For complex inputs the right-conjugate contraction \\(\\sum_i u_i\\overline{v_i}\\) differs from a bilinear product; the measured contract is stated explicitly.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/Scalar/Dot", "title": "Dot", "url": "Rodin/Math/Scalar/Dot/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/Scalar/Dot", "brief": "Scalar and one-component contraction representation measurements.", "text": "Scalar and one-component contraction representation measurements. Measured operation Measures one-component pair contractions without finite-element setup. Mathematical foundation A complete batch computes \\(D_{ba}=z_b\\overline{y_a}\\) or replaces a zeroed output by \\(D_{ba}\\mathrel{+}=wz_b\\overline{y_a}\\). Seeded scopes additionally form \\(0+z_b\\overline{y_a}\\) before storing or weighting it. These represent scalar arithmetic, fixed-size Eigen views and the public one-component spatial-vector operation. No mathematical equality of their general floating-point algorithms is assumed. Architecture Raw scalar arrays are shared by the scalar and fixed-view protocols. Spatial protocols instead retain arrays of one-component spatial values; their object footprint is reported separately. Dyadic inputs give exact finite reference values, including genuinely complex right operands and real-valued right operands representing reference basis values. A perturbed output must fail qualification. This does not certify arbitrary complex, nonfinite or rounding-mode behavior across representations. Setup and qualification are untimed. Each iteration includes pair traversal and output stores; weighted scopes also include output zeroing. Barriers surround a batch rather than each contraction. No geometry, quadrature, basis scaling, matrix scatter or solving is measured. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinScalarDotBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SparseAlgebra", "title": "SparseAlgebra", "url": "Rodin/Math/SparseAlgebra/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SparseAlgebra", "brief": "Sparse products and reductions on manufactured finite-element systems.", "text": "Sparse products and reductions on manufactured finite-element systems. Measured operation Isolates the Math operations used inside constrained CG iterations. Matrices are the actual constrained Poisson, conductivity and scalar reaction--diffusion operators, not synthetic dimension-dependent stencils. Mesh/space construction, original-kernel comparison, matrix assembly, vector allocation and numerical checks precede timing. Products overwrite a preallocated output; reductions return a scalar. These scopes exclude preconditioner setup, solver control and assembly. Numerical guards An independent serial traversal accumulates products and Hermitian dot products using long double (whose precision is reported, not assumed). Non-collinear complex vector components exercise conjugation. Manufactured coefficients separately check the assembled equation. Wrong outputs must be rejected. No timing assertion, CTest entry, storage-layout change or relaxed arithmetic is introduced. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSparseAlgebraBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialAlgebra", "title": "SpatialAlgebra", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialAlgebra/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialAlgebra", "brief": "Fixed-capacity spatial arithmetic and contraction timings.", "text": "Fixed-capacity spatial arithmetic and contraction timings. Measured operation Times spatial value operations with runtime extents and escaping results. Operand initialization and independent contraction checks precede timing. Compiler barriers expose operands and results on every iteration so neither constant folding nor dead-store removal can eliminate the measured operation. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinMathBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix", "title": "SpatialMatrix", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialMatrix", "brief": "SpatialMatrix benchmarks separate arithmetic, contractions, products and matrix decompositions. Ordinary multiplication obeys \\(C_{ij}=\\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}\\), without conjugation of complex operands. Right-conjugated scalar contractions are identified separately.", "text": "SpatialMatrix benchmarks separate arithmetic, contractions, products and matrix decompositions. Ordinary multiplication obeys \\(C_{ij}=\\sum_k A_{ik}B_{kj}\\), without conjugation of complex operands. Right-conjugated scalar contractions are identified separately. Representations and products Spatial matrices are compared with bounded, dynamic and selected fixed Eigen matrices. Matched products multiply an \\(r\\times k\\) matrix by a \\(k\\times r\\) matrix. The separate matrix-product component covers the full three-extent sweep and mixed scalar orders. Decompositions Trace, determinant, inverse, solve and full-rank pseudoinverse cases retain their shape requirements. Full-pivot LU and complete orthogonal decomposition provide untimed references. Pseudoinverse comparators retain the same normal-equation algorithm inside timing. Measurement contract Owned-result cases include materialization and assignment. Destination cases use prepared disjoint storage and a distinct result lifecycle. Inputs and numerical references are excluded; outputs are checked before and after timing. Calling-thread CPU time is reported per sweep of one or thirty-two inputs; divide by the operations counter for time per matrix.", "target": "RodinMathSpatialMatrixBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/Contraction", "title": "Contraction", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/Contraction/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialMatrix/Contraction", "brief": "Conjugating spatial-matrix contractions and weighted pair batches.", "text": "Conjugating spatial-matrix contractions and weighted pair batches. Measured operation Measures the public Frobenius product used by named elasticity. Mathematical operation For retained matrices with logical shape \\(r\\times c\\), the scalar scope evaluates \\(\\sum_{i,j} A_{ij}\\overline{B_{ij}}\\). The batch scope evaluates \\(K_{ba}=w\\sum_{i,j} A^a_{ij}\\overline{B^b_{ij}}\\) with \\(w=3/4\\) and \\(N^2\\) pairs. Output zeroing and weighted accumulation are timed. Setup, exact qualification and perturbation checks are untimed. Measurement boundary Original uses const dynamic Eigen blocks, preserving the existing public reduction expression and reading dimensions from the operands. KnownColumns selects three-column views before the batch pair loop; the row extent and total size remain dynamic to retain the original reduction strategy. Other shapes retain the public spatial-matrix dot call. This experimental caller-known shape protocol models a local assembly consumer, not an optimization of the generic spatial-matrix dot API. All scopes retain runtime dimensions and poison inactive storage. Bounded dyadic inputs admit an independent exact sum, including mixed real/complex fields and empty rectangular shapes. No operand symmetry is assumed. Counts 6, 12 and 30 cover representative vector-element pair-loop sizes, not complete finite element kernels. Geometry, coefficient evaluation, Jacobian mapping and sparse insertion are excluded. The named-form benchmark supplies the assembly consumer. Component protocols retain \\(mN\\) matrices for \\(m=rc\\) components and \\(N\\in\\{6,27,64\\}\\) scalar-basis-count proxies. ComponentSparse stores one active component \\(a\\bmod m\\) per matrix. Its independent oracle uses the component Kronecker delta, rather than reducing the input matrices. ComponentDense retains the same dimensions and pair counts, but fills every active entry. These are arithmetic inputs resembling component bases, not evaluations of a particular element or geometry. Empty shapes remain structural bounds, not physical refinement cases. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialMatrixContractionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixProduct", "title": "MatrixProduct", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixProduct/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixProduct", "brief": "Spatial matrix products underlying vector-basis gradient transforms.", "text": "Spatial matrix products underlying vector-basis gradient transforms. Measured operation Measures the canonical matrix product over retained basis-sized arrays. Mathematical operation For \\(A_a\\in\\mathbb F^{r\\times k}\\) and \\(B\\in\\mathbb G^{k\\times c}\\), the operation is \\(C_a=A_aB\\). There is no conjugation in this product. Reference Jacobians have \\(r=m\\), \\(k=d\\) and the geometric inverse has \\(c=s\\), where \\(m\\) is the field component count and \\(d,s\\) are reference and ambient dimensions. The real/real and complex/real selections therefore isolate the product used by vector-basis pullbacks in assembly. The other scalar pairs cover the same public arithmetic contract. Measurement boundary Each iteration multiplies every retained left matrix by the same right matrix, including result construction and assignment to retained output. Counts 6, 12, 30 and 81 model local vector-basis sizes; they do not identify particular geometries. No finite-element evaluation, geometric inverse, symmetric-gradient construction, coefficient weighting, pair contraction or sparse insertion is included. This is a primitive measurement, not a decomposition into additive fractions of a complete cell-kernel time. All dimensions zero through three and all real/complex scalar pairs are qualified. Bounded dyadic inputs admit an exact independent scalar-loop reference. Inactive input storage is poisoned, while inactive output must remain zero. Wrong values or dimensions must be rejected outside timing. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialMatrixProductBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixVectorProduct", "title": "MatrixVectorProduct", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixVectorProduct/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialMatrix/MatrixVectorProduct", "brief": "Runtime-shape spatial matrix-vector products used by gradient mapping.", "text": "Runtime-shape spatial matrix-vector products used by gradient mapping. Measured operation Measures the canonical fixed-capacity product with runtime extents. Architecture All rectangular shapes with zero through three rows/columns and all real/complex operand pairs are measured. Input padding is poisoned; the logical contraction must not consume it and inactive result storage must be zero. Bounded dyadic operands give an exact independent reference sum, including genuinely complex values and mixed scalar promotion. Timed calls include result construction and its storage initialization. Escaping operand/result addresses and memory barriers prevent constant folding and discarded padding stores. Guards and operand setup are untimed. The owning Variational physical-gradient benchmark measures the consumer; these primitive timings are not assembly or gradient-sweep timings. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialMatrixVectorBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector", "title": "SpatialVector", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialVector", "brief": "SpatialVector represents a small vector with runtime active dimension and bounded storage. The suite separates arithmetic, storage lifetime, scalar products, norms and cross products.", "text": "SpatialVector represents a small vector with runtime active dimension and bounded storage. The suite separates arithmetic, storage lifetime, scalar products, norms and cross products. Scalar products The right-conjugated product is \\(\\langle u,v\\rangle=\\sum_i u_i\\overline{v_i}\\). Bilinear contractions \\(\\sum_i u_iv_i\\) are separate cases. Complex cross products use the ordinary bilinear convention. Representations and result lifetime Spatial values are compared with bounded Eigen values, dynamic Eigen vectors and selected fixed vectors. Owned-result cases include materialization and assignment. Destination cases evaluate into prepared, disjoint storage and are compared separately. Compound assignments include copying the left operand. Measurement contract Inputs, numerical references and array allocation are excluded. Scalar-loop oracles check results before and after timing. Each sweep processes one or thirty-two inputs. Calling-thread CPU time is reported per sweep; divide by the operations counter for time per value. Empty shapes are retained where defined; normalization requires nonempty vectors.", "target": "RodinMathSpatialVectorBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector/Dot", "title": "Dot", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector/Dot/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialVector/Dot", "brief": "Paired public spatial-dot and original dynamic-reduction measurements.", "text": "Paired public spatial-dot and original dynamic-reduction measurements. Measured operation Measures public spatial contractions over a retained pair batch. Architecture For \\(N\\) left and right vectors of logical dimension \\(d\\), stores \\(D_{ba}=\\sum_{j=0}^{d-1}u_{bj}\\overline{v_{aj}}\\). Full batches evaluate all \\(N^2\\) pairs; lower batches evaluate \\(N(N+1)/2\\) pairs without assuming that the arbitrary input vectors define a symmetric operator. Barriers surround a complete batch, not each scalar contraction. Construction and exact entry qualification remain outside timing. The original dynamic Eigen reduction through const views supplies every reference entry. Compile-time guards reject mutable comparator views. Inactive vector storage is poisoned, including all storage at dimension zero. A changed output must fail qualification. This measures Math::dot with pair-loop and output-store overhead, not physical gradient mapping, weighted local-matrix accumulation or complete assembly. RowSnapshot scopes copy the current left vector into a local value before traversing its right-hand pairs. Ordinary Public and Original scopes keep a borrowed row. This isolates value-ownership effects without changing the reduction expression or adding a retained cache. The strategy is not an assembly timing decomposition. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialDotBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector/Storage", "title": "Storage", "url": "Rodin/Math/SpatialVector/Storage/index.html", "display": "Modules/Math/SpatialVector/Storage", "brief": "Separates fixed-capacity spatial-vector construction and storage costs.", "text": "Separates fixed-capacity spatial-vector construction and storage costs. Measured operation Measures fixed-capacity values inside a reserved owning array. Each sweep creates or assigns a batch of spatial vectors. Array allocation and source preparation are untimed; append scopes include clearing the previous batch. TemporaryFillAppend constructs a zeroed local vector, fills its active components and moves it into the array. DirectFillAppend constructs the zeroed vector in the final array slot before filling it. CopyAppend uses preconstructed immutable vectors; CopyAssign retains both the size and capacity of the destination array throughout timing. ZeroResize constructs dimension zero and then changes the logical size. These are operation scopes, not isolated instruction or allocator timings. Exact guard Every component and logical size is checked exactly before and after the timed sweep, including the unused fixed capacity. All constructor-zeroed inactive components must remain zero. A perturbed active or inactive component must be rejected. Input pointers and memory barriers prevent invariant-output hoisting. Dimensions zero through three, both scalar fields and three batch sizes separate logical work from fixed storage. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinSpatialVectorStorageBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/PETSc", "title": "PETSc", "url": "Rodin/PETSc/index.html", "display": "Modules/PETSc", "brief": "PETSc integration module for Rodin.", "text": "PETSc:: Math:: LinearSystem groups PETSc matrix and vector handles, while KSP and SNES expose linear and nonlinear solvers. PETSc-backed variational and assembly specializations connect the same weak-form interfaces to distributed algebra, preconditioning, and solver configuration for larger PDE systems.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly", "title": "Assembly", "url": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/index.html", "display": "Modules/PETSc/Assembly", "brief": "PETSc assembly.", "text": "PETSc assembly. These workloads map finite-element operators and loads into PETSc matrices and vectors. Native element calculations, sparse insertion and backend completion contribute distinct work; the leaf descriptions specify what enters each measured public operation.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Constrained", "title": "Constrained", "url": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Constrained/index.html", "display": "Modules/PETSc/Assembly/Constrained", "brief": "Full constrained PETSc local/MPI assembly, with aligned timing.", "text": "Full constrained PETSc local/MPI assembly, with aligned timing. Measured operation Measures complete PETSc problem assembly with independent patch checks. Fixed iterations align collective calls. The maximum rank duration includes constraint assembly and matrix/vector completion but excludes the preceding barrier, timing reduction, interpolation and numerical checks. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPETScConstrainedAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Mass", "title": "Mass", "url": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Mass/index.html", "display": "Modules/PETSc/Assembly/Mass", "brief": "PETSc local/MPI mass and reaction operator/load assembly.", "text": "PETSc local/MPI mass and reaction operator/load assembly. Measured operation Measures full operator/load assembly with aligned MPI collectives. Architecture Exact geometry is installed before explicit root partitioning and fixed space construction. Untimed geometry, matrix, load and constant-field checks certify each output. Three iterations align collectives; maximum-rank elapsed assembly time excludes barriers, reductions and checks. Reaction coefficients are doubled after timing to verify replacement and cache invalidation. Geometry evaluation is rank-local; its result participates in the existing explicitly global numerical oracle, with no additional collective. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPETScMassAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Physics", "title": "Physics", "url": "Rodin/PETSc/Assembly/Physics/index.html", "display": "Modules/PETSc/Assembly/Physics", "brief": "PETSc local/MPI warmed physics assembly with aligned iterations.", "text": "PETSc local/MPI warmed physics assembly with aligned iterations. Measured operation Complete warmed assembly measured independently of setup and oracles. Architecture: identical forms are configured through PhysicsForm. MPI meshes use explicit incidence and root partitioning; only owned cells contribute to workload counts. Fixed iteration counts align collectives. Assembly is timed between steady-clock readings; MPI reports maximum rank duration. Synchronization, timing reduction, and numerical checks are outside timing. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPETScPhysicsAssemblyBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/QF", "title": "Quadrature formulae", "url": "Rodin/QF/index.html", "display": "Modules/QF", "brief": "Module for defining quadrature formulae over reference polytopes.", "text": "QuadratureFormula selects persistent reference integration rules by geometry and exactness. GaussLegendre , TensorProduct , and the simplex rule families supply points and weights for element integration and reference-basis tabulation.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/QF/Quadrature", "title": "Quadrature", "url": "Rodin/QF/Quadrature/index.html", "display": "Modules/QF/Quadrature", "brief": "Reference quadrature-rule access and integration.", "text": "Reference quadrature-rule access and integration. Measured operation The quadrature families provide samples \\(\\{(\\widehat x_q,w_q)\\}\\) for reference-cell integration, \\(Q(f)=\\sum_q w_q f(\\widehat x_q)\\). Cases exercise rule construction and integration across the registered reference geometries and orders; their names distinguish these scopes. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinQFBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/QF/ReferenceQuadrature", "title": "ReferenceQuadrature", "url": "Rodin/QF/ReferenceQuadrature/index.html", "display": "Modules/QF/ReferenceQuadrature", "brief": "Profiles owned rules, traversal and controlled canonical working sets.", "text": "Profiles owned rules, traversal and controlled canonical working sets. Measured operation Owned construction and copying operate on concrete quadrature families. The move scope transfers unique ownership between two slots. These scopes no longer measure the removed dispatcher wrapper. Controlled default requests exercise the order-and-geometry lookup. The nine-request sweep misses that lookup, but normalized Gaussian point counts can still reuse a concrete-family lookup entry. Recorded results Each case records its inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinReferenceQuadratureBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Scotch", "title": "Scotch", "url": "Rodin/Scotch/index.html", "display": "Modules/Scotch", "brief": "Graph partitioning through the Scotch integration.", "text": "Partitioner builds a graph-based partition of mesh cells using Scotch. The resulting cell assignments feed mesh sharding and distribution, helping divide finite element workloads across MPI ranks while limiting connections between partitions.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Serialization", "title": "Serialization", "url": "Rodin/Serialization/index.html", "display": "Modules/Serialization", "brief": "Serialization and reconstruction of library objects.", "text": "Boost.Serialization adapters store arrays, Eigen-compatible dense matrices, flat containers, and optional values. The export registrations cover selected ParametricTransformation instantiations used in MPI shard transfer. Including the relevant adapter headers enables binary archives of supported numerical data and container structures.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solid", "title": "Solid", "url": "Rodin/Solid/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solid", "brief": "Constitutive laws, kinematics, and solid-mechanics integration.", "text": "getLogJacobian() computes deformation measures for constitutive laws such as NeoHookean and HolzapfelOgden . InternalVirtualWork assembles residuals and consistent tangents, and stress fields such as CauchyStress<Law> support postprocessing. The module covers finite-strain elasticity and fiber-reinforced material models.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solid/Integrators", "title": "Integrators", "url": "Rodin/Solid/Integrators/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solid/Integrators", "brief": "Constitutive integrator kernels.", "text": "Constitutive integrator kernels. Finite-strain element kernels evaluate internal virtual work, \\(\\int_K P(F):\\nabla v\\,dx\\), and its tangent. Law binding, trial actions and test contractions are measured separately from global sparse assembly and nonlinear solution.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solid/Integrators/InternalVirtualWorkTangent", "title": "InternalVirtualWorkTangent", "url": "Rodin/Solid/Integrators/InternalVirtualWorkTangent/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solid/Integrators/InternalVirtualWorkTangent", "brief": "CPU-time benchmarks for complete cell-local hyperelastic tangents.", "text": "CPU-time benchmarks for complete cell-local hyperelastic tangents. Measured operation Mesh, fields, quadrature and reference tabulation are prepared before timing. Each iteration binds one cell and computes all local matrix entries, including constitutive binding, tangent actions and test-gradient contractions. Sparse assembly, solving and matrix reads are excluded. Each case runs on one thread; use the CPU column and run benchmark executables serially. The postcondition checks repeatability; the unit tests supply the component-contraction oracle. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinHyperElasticityKernelBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solid/Linear", "title": "Linear", "url": "Rodin/Solid/Linear/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solid/Linear", "brief": "Linear constitutive kernels.", "text": "Linear constitutive kernels. Linear elasticity relates infinitesimal strain \\(\\varepsilon(u)=\\operatorname{sym}\\nabla u\\) to stress. These local kernels isolate constitutive and strain contractions before they enter a global elasticity operator.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solid/Linear/LinearElasticityKernel", "title": "LinearElasticityKernel", "url": "Rodin/Solid/Linear/LinearElasticityKernel/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solid/Linear/LinearElasticityKernel", "brief": "Cell-local named elasticity kernels with explicit quadrature accounting.", "text": "Cell-local named elasticity kernels with explicit quadrature accounting. Measured operation Measures one local stiffness matrix without scatter or global setup. Measurement contract Warm retains the kernel, output capacity, reference derivatives and mapped geometry. FirstKernel constructs, evaluates and destroys a kernel and its output on every iteration, with mapped geometry already warm. Neither scope represents process-first quadrature lookup or cold geometry. Exactly one selected cell is evaluated per iteration; mesh and space construction, oracle evaluation and guards remain outside timing. Native and prescribed quadratic geometry use the same unit-box mesh and full-dimensional vector space. Constant real Lam\u00e9 parameters are fixed at \\(\\lambda=2\\) and \\(\\mu=3\\). The public integral supplies an independent implementation of the same requested quadrature rule, not a claim of exact integration on curved or rational cells. The dimensionless comparison is \\(\\|A_K-A_K^{\\mathrm{integral}}\\|_F/ \\max(1,\\|A_K^{\\mathrm{integral}}\\|_F)\\le10^{-11}\\). A perturbed matrix must be rejected before timing starts. Counters distinguish factor degree, reported element order, requested quadrature order and actual point count. The pair-component work proxy is \\(n_q n_{\\mathrm{local}}^2 s^2\\); it is not an instruction count and excludes reference preparation and geometry costs. No source optimization, quadrature reduction, new cache, collective or CTest timing gate is added. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinLinearElasticityKernelBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver", "title": "Solver", "url": "Rodin/Solver/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver", "brief": "Solution of linear and nonlinear discrete systems.", "text": "CG handles symmetric positive-definite systems, GMRES handles general iterative solves, and SparseLU provides direct sparse factorization. NewtonSolver drives nonlinear solves. These solvers operate on prepared systems or variational problems and provide convergence controls for PDE and optimization calculations.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/Constrained", "title": "Constrained", "url": "Rodin/Solver/Constrained/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/Constrained", "brief": "Zero-initial-guess solves paired with constrained assembly workloads.", "text": "Zero-initial-guess solves paired with constrained assembly workloads. Measured operation Profiles solves of a fixed, assembled constrained problem. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinConstrainedSolveBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/CoupledReactionDiffusion", "title": "CoupledReactionDiffusion", "url": "Rodin/Solver/CoupledReactionDiffusion/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/CoupledReactionDiffusion", "brief": "Fixed-system CG on manufactured two-field reaction--diffusion operators.", "text": "Fixed-system CG on manufactured two-field reaction--diffusion operators. Measured operation Measures zero-initial-guess solves on one fixed coupled discretization. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinCoupledReactionDiffusionSolveBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/DiagonalPreconditioner", "title": "DiagonalPreconditioner", "url": "Rodin/Solver/DiagonalPreconditioner/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/DiagonalPreconditioner", "brief": "Diagonal-preconditioner setup and application on constrained operators.", "text": "Diagonal-preconditioner setup and application on constrained operators. Measured operation Separates diagonal setup from iteration-time application. The Eigen backend and lower/upper policy match Solver::CG's current native implementation. This executable uses that backend's preconditioner type; it does not add a source inspection API or a second Rodin solver path. Mesh/space setup, manufactured assembly and reference checks are untimed. Lifetimes OwnedCompute includes local construction, compute and destruction. RetainedCompute reuses the already-sized diagonal storage, as in repeated fixed-size CG calls. Apply overwrites a preallocated output and excludes compute. OwnedCompute is not process-first or allocator-cold measurement. Fresh and retained instances are checked before and after the timed loop. Numerical guards The independent result is \\(y_j=x_j/A_{jj}\\). These manufactured SPD systems must have finite, nonzero diagonal entries. Wrong outputs are rejected. Recomputing on \\(1.5A\\) must produce \\(y/1.5\\), followed by restoration of the original result. Thus stale retained diagonal data is not accepted. No timing gate or CTest entry is added. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinDiagonalPreconditionerBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/Helmholtz", "title": "Helmholtz", "url": "Rodin/Solver/Helmholtz/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/Helmholtz", "brief": "Zero-initial-guess GMRES on manufactured complex Helmholtz operators.", "text": "Zero-initial-guess GMRES on manufactured complex Helmholtz operators. Measured operation Measures a fixed assembled operator and records its numerical work. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinHelmholtzSolveBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/Stokes", "title": "Stokes", "url": "Rodin/Solver/Stokes/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/Stokes", "brief": "", "text": "", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Solver/Stokes/GMRESBlockSchur", "title": "GMRESBlockSchur", "url": "Rodin/Solver/Stokes/GMRESBlockSchur/index.html", "display": "Modules/Solver/Stokes/GMRESBlockSchur", "brief": "Measures the public native GMRES solver on the complete Taylor\u2013Hood Stokes system, with the original pressure-mean multiplier and unit viscosity.", "text": "Measures the public native GMRES solver on the complete Taylor\u2013Hood Stokes system, with the original pressure-mean multiplier and unit viscosity. Measured operation Each invocation starts from zero and includes fixed block-preconditioner setup and a restarted GMRES solve. The velocity block uses incomplete Cholesky; a pressure mass factorization approximates the Schur complement, with the mean constraint handled explicitly. No dense Stokes Schur matrix is constructed. Meshes, assembly and pressure mass construction are outside timing. This measurement has no residual-correction solve. Numerical qualification All original cell families, degrees and resolutions remain registered. Restart is 60, the maximum iteration count is 1200 and the backend tolerance is 1e-13. Setup and repeated solves retain the original independent coefficient-error, true-residual, affine-patch and sensitivity checks. The backend error estimate is recorded separately and does not replace the true residual. The missing-pressure control is scaled by the independently assembled forcing load on unconstrained velocity test DOFs, not by Dirichlet-elimination entries in the full right-hand side. Since the affine field has zero viscous divergence, the omitted residual must equal the negative load within the full-RHS-normalized 10 \u221211 residual tolerance, with relative magnitude above 10 \u22124 . The original full-RHS-normalized counter is retained for comparison.", "target": "RodinStokesGMRESBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Test", "title": "Test", "url": "Rodin/Test/index.html", "display": "Modules/Test", "brief": "Helpers for constructing numerical tests.", "text": "FDProbe compares assembled tangents with finite differences and returns error diagnostics in FDProbeReport . ExpectedResultTable organizes known input-output cases, and random generators supply test samples. These helpers support numerical regression tests for constitutive laws, geometry, and variational operators.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Utility", "title": "Utility", "url": "Rodin/Utility/index.html", "display": "Modules/Utility", "brief": "Type utilities and generic programming helpers.", "text": "ParameterPack and Zip support compile-time type and parameter manipulation. Overloaded combines callable visitors, and OptionalReference represents optional non-owning access. These helpers support generic expressions, type dispatch, and reusable library extensions.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational", "title": "Variational", "url": "Rodin/Variational/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational", "brief": "Finite element spaces, fields, and variational problems.", "text": "H1 and P1 define finite element spaces, while GridFunction evaluates and interpolates discrete fields. TrialFunction , TestFunction , and Problem express weak forms, boundary conditions, and the algebraic problems used to solve PDEs.", "target": "", "kind": "Module"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/CoefficientEvaluation", "title": "CoefficientEvaluation", "url": "Rodin/Variational/CoefficientEvaluation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/CoefficientEvaluation", "brief": "Generic coefficient evaluation inside weighted assembly.", "text": "Generic coefficient evaluation inside weighted assembly. Measured operation Coefficient-weighted forms evaluate their spatially varying data at integration points while constructing the operator. The registered cases vary the expression and finite-element context, exposing the combined evaluation and assembly cost. Fixture creation is outside the benchmark loop; coefficient evaluation needed by each assembly remains inside. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalCoefficientEvaluationBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/FieldContraction", "title": "FieldContraction", "url": "Rodin/Variational/FieldContraction/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/FieldContraction", "brief": "Separates field coefficient lookup from reference-gradient contraction.", "text": "Separates field coefficient lookup from reference-gradient contraction. Measured operation Measures the consumer of already evaluated reference gradients. Mathematical foundation For the cell closure \\(I_K(a)\\), the reference derivative is \\(\\widehat\\nabla u_h(\\widehat x_q)= \\sum_a c_{I_K(a)}\\widehat\\nabla\\phi_a(\\widehat x_q)\\). Coefficients are distinct deterministic values, not interpreted as nodal samples. Real and complex fields use \\(c_i=(i+1)\\eta\\), with \\(\\eta=1\\) and \\(\\eta=1+i/2\\), respectively. Architecture One cell, its space, quadrature, public basis gradients and output storage are prepared before timing. CoefficientLookup writes one coefficient per sample and basis through the public local-to-global lookup. ReferenceContraction additionally performs the ordered gradient sum. PreparedIndexContraction uses an owned closure copied before timing to isolate lookup from contraction. It is a caller-side measurement control, not a new library implementation. Physical mapping, derivative generation, assembly and solving are absent. These local scopes do not establish distributed lookup costs or borrow rules. Numerical qualification Every prepared closure index must equal the public local-to-global index. Each coefficient and contraction entry is compared exactly with an untimed closure-based ordered expansion before and after timing. Coefficients change through \\(1\\to2\\to1\\) without changing the space. Deliberately changed output must be rejected. No timing or approximation tolerance is used. This validates the lookup/contraction decomposition, not independent basis accuracy; that remains covered by element and convergence tests. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinFieldContractionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/FieldDerivative", "title": "FieldDerivative", "url": "Rodin/Variational/FieldDerivative/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/FieldDerivative", "brief": "Public grid-function differential evaluation across binding protocols.", "text": "Public grid-function differential evaluation across binding protocols. Measured operation Measures field derivatives rather than individual shape derivatives. Mathematical foundation Degree-one scopes interpolate the affine field \\(f(x)=\\eta(1+\\sum_j(j+1)x_j)\\). Higher-order scopes use \\(f(x)=\\eta(1+\\sum_j(j+1)(x_j+x_j^2))\\), whose varying derivative checks sample dependence. Vector fields have components \\(u_c=(c+1)f\\). Here \\(\\eta=1\\) or \\(1+i/2\\). Interpolation applies the space's DOF functionals; no coefficient layout or nodal-value interpretation is assumed. Analytic gradients/Jacobians, perturbation rejection and coefficient scaling \\(1\\to2\\to1\\) are checked outside timing. Repeating the timed protocol must reproduce every retained entry exactly; different protocols are not required to have identical floating-point association. Architecture The same retained cell and mapped samples are used by direct geometric-point, provenance-only integration-point and owner/index-associated protocols. Geometry, scalar reference tables, interpolation and output buffers are prepared before timing. Public differential evaluation and output assignment remain timed. This isolates the source fallback consumed by error integration without changing convergence quadrature, meshes, norms or solver requirements. Native P1 remains a separate control: an associated binding does not promise that every differential implementation consumes reference tables. These are local, opt-in component benchmarks, not CTest or CI timing gates. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinFieldDerivativeBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/FieldOperations", "title": "FieldOperations", "url": "Rodin/Variational/FieldOperations/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/FieldOperations", "brief": "Interpolation, point evaluation and L2 projection feature measurements.", "text": "Interpolation, point evaluation and L2 projection feature measurements. Measured operation Measures one field feature on an affine mesh. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinFieldOperationBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/Forms", "title": "Forms", "url": "Rodin/Variational/Forms/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/Forms", "brief": "Named mass and diffusion forms compared with equivalent integrals.", "text": "Named mass and diffusion forms compared with equivalent integrals. Measured operation Named forms and integral expressions assemble the same mass and diffusion operators on two- and three-dimensional P1 and H1 spaces. Mesh and finite-element-space construction precede timing. Each iteration reassembles the global form and exposes the stored nonzero count, so these are complete form-assembly comparisons rather than isolated scalar kernels. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalNamedFormsBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction", "title": "GridFunction", "url": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/GridFunction", "brief": "Finite-element fields.", "text": "Finite-element fields. A grid function represents a finite-element field by coefficients and evaluates the local expansion \\(u_h(x)=\\sum_a u_a\\phi_a(x)\\). Interpolation constructs those coefficients through the supported degree-of-freedom functionals; evaluation and interpolation have separate timing boundaries.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction/Evaluation", "title": "Evaluation", "url": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction/Evaluation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/GridFunction/Evaluation", "brief": "Grid-function point-evaluation benchmarks.", "text": "Grid-function point-evaluation benchmarks. Measured operation Compares finite-element expansion evaluation with the mapped-basis contraction previously used by GridFunction at arbitrary geometric points. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalGridFunctionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction/Interpolation", "title": "Interpolation", "url": "Rodin/Variational/GridFunction/Interpolation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/GridFunction/Interpolation", "brief": "Interpolation and re-interpolation benchmarks with numerical oracles.", "text": "Interpolation and re-interpolation benchmarks with numerical oracles. Measured operation Meshes, spaces, analytic functions and quadrature points are prepared before timing. Each operation checks the final value against the analytic field. Tabulated evaluation repeats one sample or alternates samples in the same cell. Cross-space assignment uses distinct spaces on the same mesh and therefore interpolates the source rather than copying its coefficients. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinGridFunctionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1", "title": "H1", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1", "brief": "High-order H1 operations.", "text": "High-order H1 operations. High-order H1 spaces use reference bases with geometry-dependent pullbacks. Basis preparation, binding, nodal contractions and local integration depend differently on degree and reference-cell geometry; the component benchmarks isolate these dependencies.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/BasisLifecycle", "title": "BasisLifecycle", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/BasisLifecycle/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/BasisLifecycle", "brief": "H1 first-binding, identity-working-set and sample-sweep driver.", "text": "H1 first-binding, identity-working-set and sample-sweep driver. Measured operation Measures reference association, identity churn and sample-sweep costs. The H1 component basis is \\(\\Phi_{a,c}=\\phi_a E_c\\). Scalar and vector controls retain the same scalar basis and component convention. Associated Single scopes bind sample zero; Sweep scopes bind every mapped sample. AssociatedFresh measures one first binding before any numerical warmup; its direct checks run afterwards. AssociatedOne repeats one owned formula. AssociatedNine cycles nine distinct formula identities, targeting the current eight-entry reference store. UnprovenNine uses the same owned points but only origin metadata, which must never authorize reference-table substitution. Unproven Single binds the last sample while origin metadata remains index zero; Unproven Sweep likewise retains origin index zero while actual coordinates vary. Measurement boundary Mesh, space, formulas and mapped samples are constructed outside timing. Except for AssociatedFresh, two untimed priming cycles establish the identity protocol. Fresh scopes force one iteration and one repetition; a controlled process-first study must additionally select one case per new process. The timed operation includes identity selection, binding and all active-value stores. Identity churn can include allocation, value and derivative tabulation for implementations using the reference store. It is not process-first timing; shared element initialization is already warm. No query-count instrumentation is inserted into timed formula access. Public table-presence queries occur only between priming cycles and report participation rather than requiring a particular implementation to use the cache. Numerical qualification Every output is checked exactly against direct scalar basis evaluation. Logical dimensions and reference authority are checked outside timing; Fresh scopes perform all numerical qualification after the first timed bind. Changed output must fail. Only one active-value output buffer is retained. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1BasisLifecycleBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/Convection", "title": "Convection", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/Convection/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/Convection", "brief": "Measures H1 directional-gradient preparation and local contraction.", "text": "Measures H1 directional-gradient preparation and local contraction. Measured operation Mathematical foundation The measured form is \\(a_b(u,v)=\\int_K(Ju)b\\cdot\\overline v\\,dx\\). Fields are real or complex; complex fields admit either real or genuinely complex directions. Real fields use real directions, as required by the current shape-Dot range constraint. An independent expression-entry quadrature loop and a deliberate perturbation qualify every matrix before timing. The final matrix must repeat exactly. A defective baseline is not timed for the unsupported coefficient semantics. Architecture Binding includes owned mapped quadrature, scalar tabulation lookup, physical gradients and local contraction. Traversal, insertion and solving are absent. Warm scopes reuse one order; Refresh alternates two orders on the same owner. Degree, components, quadrature samples and both physical dimensions are reported separately. This component executable is opt-in and outside CTest. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1ConvectionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/MassBinding", "title": "MassBinding", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/MassBinding/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/MassBinding", "brief": "Measures the MassBinding workload through its public library pipeline.", "text": "Measures the MassBinding workload through its public library pipeline. Measured operation Measures scalar-weighted H1 mass preparation and component contraction. Mathematical foundation For scalar weight \\(\\rho\\), the discrete component entries satisfy \\[ A_{bm+e,am+c}=\\delta_{ec}S_{ba},\\qquad S_{ba}=\\sum_q w_q D_q\\rho(x_q)\\phi_a(x_q)\\overline{\\phi_b(x_q)}. \\] Matrix fields use their local row-major component convention and the Frobenius product. Unit mass uses \\(\\rho=1\\). Weighted cases use \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0)\\) for real fields and \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0+i/2)\\) for complex fields. This is not a Hermitian symmetry claim when the coefficient is complex. Measurement boundary One-cell public binding includes mapped quadrature, scalar reference-data lookup and complete local matrix preparation. Construction, independent qualification and final repeated-entry checks are untimed. Scatter, load assembly and projection solves belong to their owning consumer modules. The original expression-entry loop qualifies only the scalar block. Every component entry is then checked exactly against the retained first block, including structural zeros. A perturbed scalar block must be rejected. The reference storage is therefore scalar-sized, not a second full component matrix. Doubling the borrowed coefficient scale after timing checks replacement rather than accumulation of stale operator values. Explicit orders define discrete operators; underintegrated samples are not claims about polynomial integration exactness or PDE convergence. Scalar weights are measured in both operand orders. Although \\(\\rho u=u\\rho\\) algebraically, their expression types and local quadrature dispatch may differ. The dispatch counter records this implementation property without requiring one particular implementation. General matrix coefficients are not commuted. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1MassBindingBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/MassOperandOrder", "title": "MassOperandOrder", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/MassOperandOrder/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/MassOperandOrder", "brief": "Registers both scalar-weighted expression orders without rewriting them.", "text": "Registers both scalar-weighted expression orders without rewriting them. Measured operation Measures scalar-weighted H1 mass preparation and component contraction. Mathematical foundation For scalar weight \\(\\rho\\), the discrete component entries satisfy \\[ A_{bm+e,am+c}=\\delta_{ec}S_{ba},\\qquad S_{ba}=\\sum_q w_q D_q\\rho(x_q)\\phi_a(x_q)\\overline{\\phi_b(x_q)}. \\] Matrix fields use their local row-major component convention and the Frobenius product. Unit mass uses \\(\\rho=1\\). Weighted cases use \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0)\\) for real fields and \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0+i/2)\\) for complex fields. This is not a Hermitian symmetry claim when the coefficient is complex. Measurement boundary One-cell public binding includes mapped quadrature, scalar reference-data lookup and complete local matrix preparation. Construction, independent qualification and final repeated-entry checks are untimed. Scatter, load assembly and projection solves belong to their owning consumer modules. The original expression-entry loop qualifies only the scalar block. Every component entry is then checked exactly against the retained first block, including structural zeros. A perturbed scalar block must be rejected. The reference storage is therefore scalar-sized, not a second full component matrix. Doubling the borrowed coefficient scale after timing checks replacement rather than accumulation of stale operator values. Explicit orders define discrete operators; underintegrated samples are not claims about polynomial integration exactness or PDE convergence. Scalar weights are measured in both operand orders. Although \\(\\rho u=u\\rho\\) algebraically, their expression types and local quadrature dispatch may differ. The dispatch counter records this implementation property without requiring one particular implementation. General matrix coefficients are not commuted. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1MassOperandOrderBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/PreparedBasis", "title": "PreparedBasis", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/PreparedBasis/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/PreparedBasis", "brief": "Measures H1 basis consumption with certified reference-table readiness.", "text": "Measures H1 basis consumption with certified reference-table readiness. Measured operation Measures leaf and weighted basis production through public expression nodes. Mathematical foundation For each mapped sample \\(x_q\\) and local basis index \\(a\\), stores \\(\\phi_a(x_q)\\), \\(\\rho(x_q)\\phi_a(x_q)\\) or \\(\\phi_a(x_q)\\rho(x_q)\\), respectively. The scalar coefficient is \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0)\\) over the reals and \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0+i/2)\\) over the complex numbers. Point uses \\(x_0=0\\). Component values are stored in quadrature/basis/component order, with row-major logical components for matrices. This is an output layout, not an assumption about global DOF numbering or nodal interpolation. Measurement boundary One sweep includes integration-point rebinding, coefficient-cache binding, public getBasis calls and active-value stores. Mesh/space/node construction, mapped quadrature and public-binding priming are outside timing. Binding priming alone does not guarantee reference-table readiness for matrix leaves. Prepared-reference scopes explicitly construct the scalar table before priming and require its presence immediately before and after the measured loop. Preparation is not included in their reported consumption time. No basis pairs, Frobenius contractions, weighted integration, scatter or solving are included. The leaf control is a separate operation, not a timing to subtract to obtain coefficient or allocation cost. All node borrows refer to one owned integration-point context that outlives the nodes. Numerical qualification Returned logical vector and matrix dimensions are checked outside timing. Every output is compared exactly with the public unweighted basis and the corresponding scalar multiplication in its original operand order. This isolates materialisation, not independent basis accuracy. Changed output must be rejected. Coefficient scales \\(1\\to2\\to1\\) exercise rebinding without changing logical sample identities. Only one flattened active-value output is retained; a second full basis-value table is not constructed. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1PreparedBasisBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/Pyramid", "title": "Pyramid", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/Pyramid/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/Pyramid", "brief": "Separates pyramid index, Bernstein and modal source-kernel costs.", "text": "Separates pyramid index, Bernstein and modal source-kernel costs. Measured operation Attributes intrinsic real pyramid kernels without nodal contraction. Metadata, Bernstein factors and modal evaluation are measured separately. No Vandermonde inverse, reference-table lookup, map, mesh, assembly or solve is timed. Degrees one through six cover the supported performance matrix. Factor and modal sweeps use orders two, four and eight; metadata sweeps use their degree-dependent index sets instead of meaningless quadrature sizes. Runtime argument arrays are exposed through compiler barriers before each sweep so that constant loop bounds cannot turn binomial or index decoding into precomputed outputs. The barrier and output-write costs remain in the reported sweep time; these are not isolated instruction latencies. Independent numerical oracle Binomial coefficients are constructed by Pascal addition, independently of the source's ordered product/division. Factors use \\(B_i^n(t)=\\binom ni t^i(1-t)^{n-i}\\) and direct polynomial differentiation, evaluated with powers rather than the production multiplication loops. With \\(a=x/(1-z)\\), \\(b=y/(1-z)\\), the modal oracle is \\(B_i^{K-k}(a)B_j^{K-k}(b)B_k^K(z)\\); its gradient follows by the chain rule. Mode triples are enumerated independently in layer order, not decoded through the measured implementation. An absolute/relative roundoff budget of \\(10^{-12}\\max(1,|q_{\\rm ref}|)\\) applies to dimensionless values. Metadata comparisons are exact, and a perturbed output must be rejected. Real kernels also underlie complex finite-element coefficients; complex expansion and nodal contraction belong to the reference-element profiler. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1PyramidBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/H1/PyramidNodal", "title": "PyramidNodal", "url": "Rodin/Variational/H1/PyramidNodal/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/H1/PyramidNodal", "brief": "Controlled nodal derivative contractions with and without modal reuse.", "text": "Controlled nodal derivative contractions with and without modal reuse. Measured operation Measures a caller-side reuse experiment without changing the library. Operation Nodal derivatives are \\(\\partial_i\\phi_a(\\widehat x_q)= \\sum_b(V^{-1})_{ba}\\partial_i\\psi_b(\\widehat x_q)\\). Public calls the existing element derivative. RepeatedModes spells the same contraction with production modal derivatives. SharedModes computes each modal derivative once per point and reuses it across nodal functions. Every nodal sum retains ascending mode order; no Eigen reduction or reassociation is substituted. Nodal sums use real arithmetic before promotion to real or complex storage, matching the public element's real-valued reference basis contract. Measurement boundary Vandermonde initialization, quadrature lookup, output allocation and scratch allocation are untimed. Modal evaluation and nodal contractions are timed, including scratch writes for SharedModes. The scratch holds only one point's modal derivatives and is not a new production cache. Reference-table lookup, physical mapping, assembly and solving are absent. Public and RepeatedModes are separate compiler/calling-protocol controls; a gain in this experiment is not yet a library or assembly speedup. Numerical guards Every quadrature output must exactly match the public derivative before and after timing. Independent DOF-functional reconstruction recovers the gradient of \\(\\eta(1+x+2y+3z)\\), with \\(\\eta=1\\) for real fields and \\(\\eta=1+i/2\\) for complex fields, to an absolute dimensionless roundoff budget of \\(10^{-8}\\) at interior samples. Perturbation must be rejected. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinH1PyramidNodalBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/Integrators", "title": "Integrators", "url": "Rodin/Variational/Integrators/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/Integrators", "brief": "Finite-element integrator kernels and their assembly consumers.", "text": "Finite-element integrator kernels and their assembly consumers. Measured operation Cases compare local mass and diffusion integrator entry evaluation with the corresponding global assembly operations. Trial and test spaces, geometry and polynomial order identify each workload. Kernel preparation and global scatter are distinct scopes in the registered case names; timings from those scopes should be compared separately. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalIntegratorsBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/Mult", "title": "Mult", "url": "Rodin/Variational/Mult/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/Mult", "brief": "Public scalar-weighted basis production benchmark entry point.", "text": "Public scalar-weighted basis production benchmark entry point. Measured operation Measures leaf and weighted basis production through public expression nodes. Mathematical foundation For each mapped sample \\(x_q\\) and local basis index \\(a\\), stores \\(\\phi_a(x_q)\\), \\(\\rho(x_q)\\phi_a(x_q)\\) or \\(\\phi_a(x_q)\\rho(x_q)\\), respectively. The scalar coefficient is \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0)\\) over the reals and \\(\\rho=s(2+x_0+i/2)\\) over the complex numbers. Point uses \\(x_0=0\\). Component values are stored in quadrature/basis/component order, with row-major logical components for matrices. This is an output layout, not an assumption about global DOF numbering or nodal interpolation. Measurement boundary One sweep includes integration-point rebinding, coefficient-cache binding, public getBasis calls and active-value stores. Mesh/space/node construction, mapped quadrature and public-binding priming are outside timing. Binding priming alone does not guarantee reference-table readiness for matrix leaves. Prepared-reference scopes explicitly construct the scalar table before priming and require its presence immediately before and after the measured loop. Preparation is not included in their reported consumption time. No basis pairs, Frobenius contractions, weighted integration, scatter or solving are included. The leaf control is a separate operation, not a timing to subtract to obtain coefficient or allocation cost. All node borrows refer to one owned integration-point context that outlives the nodes. Numerical qualification Returned logical vector and matrix dimensions are checked outside timing. Every output is compared exactly with the public unweighted basis and the corresponding scalar multiplication in its original operand order. This isolates materialisation, not independent basis accuracy. Changed output must be rejected. Coefficient scales \\(1\\to2\\to1\\) exercise rebinding without changing logical sample identities. Only one flattened active-value output is retained; a second full basis-value table is not constructed. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinBasisScalingBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/NamedFormEvaluation", "title": "NamedFormEvaluation", "url": "Rodin/Variational/NamedFormEvaluation/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/NamedFormEvaluation", "brief": "Existing relative Frobenius budget for the assembled operator oracle.", "text": "Existing relative Frobenius budget for the assembled operator oracle. Measured operation Measures complete assembly against a frozen full-matrix oracle. Contexts are scalar mass, diffusion, Helmholtz, vector mass and linear elasticity, with constant or represented field coefficients. First includes form construction, assembly and destruction; Warm retains the form. Ordinary scopes retain mapped geometry. ColdGeometry flushes the mesh's transformation and mapped-quadrature caches before each assembly, outside timing. It includes lazy geometry reconstruction, not merely index-slot allocation, and does not alter topology, spaces or coefficients. These controls must not be interpreted as pure cache allocation latencies. PauseOnly uses identical timer boundaries without changing mesh state, providing a negative control for the measurement protocol. Every final sparse operator is compared with the same precomputed Integral oracle. No solve or error integration is timed. Profilers are opt-in and are not CTest or CI timing gates. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinNamedFormBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/NamedMassKernel", "title": "NamedMassKernel", "url": "Rodin/Variational/NamedMassKernel/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/NamedMassKernel", "brief": "Named mass-kernel lifecycle and within-assembly reference reuse.", "text": "Named mass-kernel lifecycle and within-assembly reference reuse. Measured operation Measures the production named mass kernel without global assembly. Mathematical foundation For the component basis \\(\\Phi_{a,c}=\\phi_a E_c\\), the local matrix is \\(A_{bm+e,am+c}=\\delta_{ec}S_{ba}\\). The scalar block is evaluated independently by the original expression-entry quadrature loop. Weighted scopes use \\(\\rho=s\\alpha\\), with \\(\\alpha=2\\) over the reals and \\(\\alpha=2+i/2\\) over the complex numbers. The borrowed scale is one during timing and two during the final coefficient-replacement check. Unweighted scopes use \\(\\rho=1\\). A complex coefficient does not imply Hermitian symmetry. The requested order is the production basis-order sum; comparison certifies that discrete operator, not rational-pyramid exactness. Architecture and measurement boundary FreshKernel includes construction, coefficient cloning where applicable, reference-cache creation, computation and kernel destruction. Output storage is retained. CachedKernel retains the same kernel and its prepared reference data across computations. This models reuse across cells within one named assembly, not retention of that cache across MassForm::assemble calls. Geometry and canonical quadrature caches are primed in both scopes. Mesh/space construction, the scalar oracle and numerical checks are untimed; scatter, constraints, solving and process-first geometry are not measured. Reference-cache payload is a source-derived model, not measured resident memory. Degrees one through three bound this initial profiling matrix; the high-degree component-expanded cache is investigated separately before launching its multi-gigabyte configurations. Point cases are structural counting-measure operations, not refinement studies. Empty shapes, mixed degrees, other space families and curved/embedded cells are separate scopes. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinNamedMassKernelBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/P1", "title": "P1", "url": "Rodin/Variational/P1/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/P1", "brief": "P1 finite element benchmark coverage", "text": "P1 finite element benchmark coverage Measured operation Benchmarks P1 finite-element-space construction and grid-function projection on hand-built and uniform triangular meshes. The cases measure how mesh size affects space setup and projection of coordinate-dependent scalar fields. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalP1Benchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/P1/QuadratureBinding", "title": "QuadratureBinding", "url": "Rodin/Variational/P1/QuadratureBinding/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/P1/QuadratureBinding", "brief": "Profiles P1 mass and transport binding with warm and refreshed scalar data.", "text": "Profiles P1 mass and transport binding with warm and refreshed scalar data. Measured operation Measures the local binding stages consumed by P1 form assembly. Mathematical foundation On one mapped cell, the forms are \\((u,v)_K\\), \\((2u,v)_K\\) and \\((J(u)b,v)_K\\), with real \\(b_j=j+1\\). The vector field has zero through three components, independently of the spatial dimension. Real and complex fields use the same real transport coefficient. Empty ranges have no matrix entries and are not assigned a fictitious entry read. Architecture Warm scopes retain scalar reference data at one integration order. Refresh scopes alternate that order and the order four degrees higher, forcing the existing owner's preparation stage without constructing a second cache. Both include owned mapped quadrature and local contraction; neither measures mesh traversal, global insertion or solving. Three base orders study local sample-count scaling, not mesh-refinement convergence. Point formulas always retain one sample and are not assigned artificial refinement rates. Independent entry-loop qualification and perturbation rejection precede timing. Repeated binding must reproduce every retained entry exactly at the final order. Empty-range qualification uses logical DOF counts instead. Benchmarks are opt-in and are not CTest or publication timing gates. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinP1QuadratureBindingBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/PhysicalGradient", "title": "PhysicalGradient", "url": "Rodin/Variational/PhysicalGradient/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/PhysicalGradient", "brief": "Reference-to-physical derivative preparation through H1 expressions.", "text": "Reference-to-physical derivative preparation through H1 expressions. Measured operation Measures public physical derivative expressions used by generic integrators. Architecture One retained affine cell and its owned mapped quadrature are used. The inverse Jacobians and reference table are warmed before timing. Changing the integration-point index requires physical-gradient preparation at every sample, not a repeated-point cache-hit measurement. Basis extraction and mesh/element queries inside the expression remain included; mapped quadrature construction, local matrix contraction and assembly are not. Specialized diffusion rules perform their own transformation loops, so this expression scope is not a timing decomposition of those rules. An affine physical field \\(f(x)=\\eta(1+\\sum_j(j+1)x_j)\\) is represented through element DOF functionals. The scalar gradient must recover \\(\\eta(1,\\ldots,d)\\). Vector scopes use \\(u_c=(c+1)f\\), with \\((\\nabla u)_{cj}=\\eta(c+1)(j+1)\\) and \\(\\operatorname{div}u=\\eta\\sum_j(j+1)^2\\). Perturbing one contributing basis must fail. Every physical basis component is also retained before timing and must remain exactly unchanged after the sweep. Native P1 scopes additionally compare every indexed basis derivative with fresh direct-point evaluation. Identity-map scopes distinguish geometric map order from reference-basis sample dependence. On affine simplices the warmed native P1 cache is intentionally reused throughout timing; the transform-product counter is a logical work proxy, not an instruction count. Native P1 basis access is materialized into an owned value before the mutable benchmark barrier; no deprecated const-reference barrier is used. The retained H1 value-return protocol is unchanged. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinPhysicalGradientBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/Problem", "title": "Problem", "url": "Rodin/Variational/Problem/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/Problem", "brief": "Problem lifecycle.", "text": "Problem lifecycle. A variational problem owns the assembly and solution sequence for its forms and constraints. Lifecycle benchmarks distinguish initial preparation from retained-layout reuse, with the same numerical system on each comparison.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/Problem/Lifecycle", "title": "Lifecycle", "url": "Rodin/Variational/Problem/Lifecycle/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/Problem/Lifecycle", "brief": "Construction and ownership transfer of a coupled variational problem.", "text": "Construction and ownership transfer of a coupled variational problem. Measured operation Measures public problem lifetimes, excluding mesh/space construction. Architecture Degree-two real/complex coupled reaction--diffusion fixtures supply the same formulation as the assembly/solve profilers. Sparse and dense native systems are distinguished. Empty and Defined include construction and destruction, with Defined also cloning the body. Copy includes copying the assembled system and owned body. Move starts from a fresh untimed copy; moved-result destruction is timed, moved-from cleanup is not. These are warmed allocator lifetimes, not process-first allocation. Assignment retains an existing destination: copy assignment may reuse fixed-size system buffers; move assignment replaces the previous value. Fresh staging copies and their moved-from cleanup remain untimed. Numerical qualification assembles a transferred body before and after measurement and compares the operator/load exactly with the fixture. Empty construction is qualified by subsequently assigning that body. A wrong empty body must fail the logical integrator-count guard. No PDE assembly, interpolation or numerical guard is included in lifecycle timing. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinProblemLifecycleBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/QuadratureBinding", "title": "QuadratureBinding", "url": "Rodin/Variational/QuadratureBinding/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/QuadratureBinding", "brief": "Local binding of the operator terms consumed by coupled assembly.", "text": "Local binding of the operator terms consumed by coupled assembly. Measured operation Profiles complete local preparation through public integral binding. Architecture Each scope binds one of the four distinct operator types in the coupled reaction-diffusion workload. It includes formula selection, owned mapped quadrature, basis preparation and local matrix contraction; it excludes global insertion, constraints and solving. The mesh, element degrees and order match the Assembly-owned consumer. Reference-table first use and numerical checks are outside timing. Mesh traversal remains included. Every cell matrix is qualified against the existing original entry-loop oracle, which shares mapped geometry but not production basis reuse. A perturbed entry must fail that comparison. A separate untimed sweep after measurement must reproduce every initially retained entry exactly. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinQuadratureBindingBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/QuadratureExpressions", "title": "QuadratureExpressions", "url": "Rodin/Variational/QuadratureExpressions/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/QuadratureExpressions", "brief": "Expression binding with prepared quadrature compared with original loops.", "text": "Expression binding with prepared quadrature compared with original loops. Measured operation Paired cases evaluate equivalent expression trees using retained quadrature binding or the original entry loop. The registered input and binding variants distinguish rule preparation from repeated evaluation. Both sides retain the same physical operation, so the comparison measures expression reuse rather than a changed quadrature formula. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinQuadratureExpressionBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/RankOneJacobian", "title": "RankOneJacobian", "url": "Rodin/Variational/RankOneJacobian/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/RankOneJacobian", "brief": "Specialized rank-one Jacobian assembly compared with generic dispatch.", "text": "Specialized rank-one Jacobian assembly compared with generic dispatch. Measured operation The form contracts coefficient-weighted trial and test Jacobians. Identity coefficients with identical types select the specialized quadrature rule; a numerically identical coefficient with a distinct type selects the generic rule. Both routes assemble the same operator, separating dispatch and contraction costs from the mathematical problem. Mesh, space and form construction precede timing; repeated assembly is included. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinVariationalRankOneJacobianBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/ReferenceElement", "title": "ReferenceElement", "url": "Rodin/Variational/ReferenceElement/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/ReferenceElement", "brief": "Isolates scalar reference-element dispatch, evaluation and tabulation.", "text": "Isolates scalar reference-element dispatch, evaluation and tabulation. Measured operation Measures one reference operation with explicit dispatch/cache scope. Direct and prefetched sweeps write the same quadrature-major output. The former obtains each descriptor in the sample loop; the latter obtains descriptors before timing. Neither path changes the library implementation. BulkDerivatives uses the element's typed gradient output, including its coordinate validation and owned point-local modal work. Its output has the same quadrature-major layout, without physical mapping or field contraction. WarmTable includes lookup only, not a sweep over table entries. Misses rotate nine independently identified formulas through the eight-entry reference cache. Initial allocation and basis initialization are outside timing; CapacityWarmMiss therefore measures rebuilding, not first-use allocation. WarmTable records its one-based search position outside timing: resident table addresses order the cache's array slots, not formula identities. WarmTablePosition selects each of the eight resident search positions explicitly before timing. Formula contents are identical across versions; lookup continues to use logical identity and geometry, never an address key. Numerical qualification Every value and derivative is compared exactly against direct evaluation before and after timing. Separately, the public DOF functionals reconstruct \\(f(\\widehat x)=1+\\sum_i(i+1)\\widehat x_i\\) and its gradient in positive-degree spaces; constant spaces reconstruct \\(f=1\\). This avoids assuming nodal coefficients for modal elements. The reconstruction tolerance is an absolute roundoff allowance on a dimensionless reference cell, not a performance acceptance threshold. A perturbed output must be rejected. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinReferenceElementBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Rodin/Variational/StokesBinding", "title": "StokesBinding", "url": "Rodin/Variational/StokesBinding/index.html", "display": "Modules/Variational/StokesBinding", "brief": "Isolates the three local operator kernels consumed by mixed Stokes assembly.", "text": "Isolates the three local operator kernels consumed by mixed Stokes assembly. Measured operation Architecture The terms are \\((\\nabla u,\\nabla v)\\), \\((\\nabla\\cdot u,q)\\) and \\((p,\\nabla\\cdot v)\\), with velocity degree K and pressure degree K-1. Public integral binding includes owned mapped quadrature, basis transforms and eager local contraction. Mesh traversal and one entry read remain timed; global insertion, constraints and solving do not. Order 16 remains the default and matches the Assembly-owned Stokes workload. Separately named boundary studies hold each mesh and mixed pair fixed while varying only the integration order; they do not change convergence tests. The original generic entry loop qualifies every cell matrix before timing; an entry perturbation must be rejected. A final sweep must reproduce each retained entry exactly. Both mixed orientations are measured rather than inferred from transposition. or three for explicitly unit-weighted vector diffusion. Recorded results Each case retains its original inputs, iteration policy and correctness checks. Raw reports preserve counters, repetitions and the Google Benchmark context. CPU, wall and manually supplied times are labeled separately; unlike operations and configurations are not averaged together.", "target": "RodinStokesBindingBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}, {"path": "Stokes", "title": "Stokes", "url": "Stokes/index.html", "display": "Stokes", "brief": "The stationary Stokes model describes slow viscous flow of an incompressible Newtonian fluid, with inertia neglected. In a domain \\(\\Omega\\), the velocity \\(\\boldsymbol{u}\\) and pressure \\(p\\) satisfy momentum balance and incompressibility:", "text": "The stationary Stokes model describes slow viscous flow of an incompressible Newtonian fluid, with inertia neglected. In a domain \\(\\Omega\\), the velocity \\(\\boldsymbol{u}\\) and pressure \\(p\\) satisfy momentum balance and incompressibility: \\[ -\\nu\\Delta\\boldsymbol{u}+\\nabla p=\\boldsymbol{f}, \\qquad \\nabla\\cdot\\boldsymbol{u}=0. \\] Here \\(\\nu>0\\) is the constant viscosity coefficient and \\(\\boldsymbol{f}\\) is the applied force. Pressure enforces the incompressibility constraint. With homogeneous velocity prescribed on \\(\\partial\\Omega\\), pressure is determined up to an additive constant; the normalization \\(\\int_\\Omega p\\,\\mathrm dx=0\\) fixes that constant. The mixed velocity\u2013pressure discretization must preserve this coupling to give a stable approximation.", "target": "", "kind": "Collection"}, {"path": "Stokes/InfSupStability", "title": "InfSupStability", "url": "Stokes/InfSupStability/index.html", "display": "Stokes/InfSupStability", "brief": "Measures the finite-mesh stability of the Stokes velocity\u2013pressure coupling through independently checked spectral calculations.", "text": "Measures the finite-mesh stability of the Stokes velocity\u2013pressure coupling through independently checked spectral calculations. A mixed discretization must control every nonconstant pressure mode through the discrete divergence pairing. The finite-mesh stability constant is \\(\\beta_h=\\inf_{q_h\\ne0}\\sup_{v_h\\ne0}|b(v_h,q_h)|/(\\|v_h\\|_V\\|q_h\\|_Q)\\) on the zero-mean pressure and essential-boundary velocity spaces. Discretizations Velocity degrees two, three and four are paired with pressure degree one lower on six cell geometries, affine and quadratic maps, several mesh resolutions, and quadrature orders sixteen and twenty. Velocity gradient energy, divergence and pressure mass matrices are assembled before timing, with unit viscosity in the energy convention. Generalized pressure spectrum Essential velocity degrees of freedom are removed. If \\(c\\) represents constant pressure, a basis \\(T\\) satisfying \\(T^{\\mathsf T}Mc=0\\) defines \\(M_0=T^{\\mathsf T}MT\\) and \\(S_0=T^{\\mathsf T}BA^{-1}B^{\\mathsf T}T\\). The generalized spectrum solves \\(S_0z_i=\\lambda_iM_0z_i\\); its smallest eigenvalue gives \\(\\beta_h^2\\) for the stated velocity and pressure norms. Generalized Schur spectrum Sparse LDLT factorization and blocked solves form the pressure-sized Schur operator without explicitly computing an inverse. A generalized self-adjoint eigensolver obtains its spectrum in the pressure mass metric. Independent singular-value route Independent Cholesky factorization and pressure whitening give \\(D=C^{-1}T^{\\mathsf T}BP^{\\mathsf T}L^{-\\mathsf T}\\), where \\(PAP^{\\mathsf T}=LL^{\\mathsf T}\\) and \\(M_0=CC^{\\mathsf T}\\). Incremental Givens QR retains a pressure-sized triangular factor for the SVD; squared singular values are compared with the Schur eigenvalues. The full dense velocity\u2013pressure coupling is not retained. Measurement contract Each timed invocation includes reduction, the zero-mean basis, both spectral routes, and residual diagnostics. Mesh construction, assembly, constant-pressure interpolation and outer warmup/repeat checks are excluded. Wall time is reported. The calculation is rank-local on complete matrices, with no MPI collectives. These finite spectra do not prove mesh-uniform inf-sup stability.", "target": "RodinMixedStabilityBenchmarks", "kind": "Benchmark"}];
